  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThe upper-level pattern consists of a high amplitude ridge over \nthe western CONUS with a trough digging into the eastern CONUS. \nWithin the trough, several shortwaves are propagating through \nthe flow, one of which is currently over the upper- and mid-\nMississippi River Valley as of 19Z. At the surface, a low \npressure system is tied to this shortwave and is centered just \nsouth of Lake Michigan. The cold front that is associated with \nthis system passed through northern MO early this morning and\nhas resulted in winds being out of the northwest. Despite the \ncold front having already passed through, diurnal mixing and\nclear skies have allowed temperatures to climb into the 60s \nthis afternoon. With high pressure to the west, the pressure \ngradient over the CWA is fairly tight and is resulting in breezy\nconditions, with sustained winds of 15-20 mph and gusts to \n25-30 mph. This is expected to continue through sunset this \nevening when mixing subsides and the low pressure system drifts \nfar enough east to relax the pressure gradient overhead.\n\nAnother shortwave is expected to move through the pattern\ntonight into tomorrow, dropping another cold front towards the\narea. As the cold front approaches the area, low-level moisture\nahead of the front will keep RH values near 100% despite an \ninflux of mid-level dry air. While the best forcing will remain\neast of the area, closer to the core of the shortwave, enough \nlift may (25-30% chance) be present across northern MO to \nsqueeze some sprinkles, or perhaps a bit of drizzle, out of the \nlow-level clouds that are expected to be present tomorrow \nmorning. The most favorable timing for this looks to be between \n6 AM and 12 PM for areas along and east of I-29/I-49. As the \nshortwave continues to move southeast, forcing will subside and \nthe forecast returns to a dry state tomorrow afternoon. This \nsecond cold front will also bring another shot of cooler air to \nthe area, which will help to limit high temperatures to the \nupper 40s and lower 50s tomorrow.\n\nHigh pressure begins to build into the Lower Missouri River\nValley tomorrow afternoon, which will allow winds to become very\nlight (less than 5 mph) and skies to clear through the \novernight period. Temperatures are expected to drop into the\nupper 20s to around 30F across northern MO by Friday morning. \nThis will likely result in the first hard freeze of the year, \nhowever, with the growing season already behind us, no \nFrost/Freeze products will be issued. This will still be a \nsituation to protect any sensitive outdoor plants despite the \nlack of any headlines.\n\nThe upper-level trough is expected to remain parked over the\neastern CONUS through the weekend resulting in a rather\nconsistent forecast from day to day. Highs will largely be in\nthe upper 40s and lower 50s each day, with lows dropping into \nthe upper 20s and lower 30s each night. This forecast is 5-10 \ndegrees below the seasonal normals for this time of year.\n\nThe next chance for precipitation comes Sunday afternoon into \nMonday morning (20-30% along and south of the MO River) as a \nshortwave moves across the Central Plains. Model guidance has \nbeen shifting this feature further south with each run, so \ntrends will need to be monitored over the coming forecast cycles\nto determine the exact placement of any precipitation.\n\nMid-level ridging is expected to shift over the central CONUS\nearly next week, bringing height rises and low-level WAA to the\narea. This will allow temperatures to climb back towards, or\nsightly above, seasonal normals (mid-50s) through the first \nhalf of next week.",
